WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.175
Second table: the Stock Log.

00:00:03.788 --> 00:00:07.100
This is where Stocky will write
down every stock check it does.

00:00:07.713 --> 00:00:11.185
I click the plus sign at the bottom,
rename the tab "Stock underscore

00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:16.998
Log", and add the headers: log ID,
log date, SKU, quantity on hand,

00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:22.662
unit, reorder point, status,
supplier ID, logged by, and note.

00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:24.613
Bold and freeze.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.340
A log is like a ship's logbook or a diary.

00:00:28.952 --> 00:00:31.027
You never rub out yesterday's entry.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.615
Every check gets a brand-new row.

00:00:34.228 --> 00:00:38.363
That way, three months from now,
Sarah can ask, "How fast were we

00:00:38.402 --> 00:00:40.738
burning through safety gloves in October?"

00:00:40.777 --> 00:00:42.853
and the answer is sitting right there.

00:00:44.024 --> 00:00:45.699
Let me add three entries.

00:00:46.311 --> 00:00:53.011
LOG-0001, today's date,
BL-1005, the pallet jack wheels.

00:00:53.624 --> 00:00:57.336
Quantity: 18. Unit: set.

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:04.236
Reorder point: 25. Supplier: SUP-004.

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:09.359
Logged by Sarah. And in the note
column: "3 pallet jacks waiting for

00:01:09.399 --> 00:01:10.337
wheels."

00:01:11.498 --> 00:01:14.073
Now pay close attention
to the quantity cell.

00:01:14.685 --> 00:01:17.761
I typed "18". Just the number.

00:01:18.373 --> 00:01:22.448
Not "18 sets". Not "18 urgent!".

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.861
The unit has its own column.

00:01:25.473 --> 00:01:27.185
The comment has its own column.

00:01:27.798 --> 00:01:31.823
This is the most important rule in
this whole video: one fact per cell.

00:01:32.996 --> 00:01:36.171
Why? Because "18" is a number.

00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:40.808
You can add it, compare it,
and ask "is it below 25?".

00:01:41.421 --> 00:01:43.256
But "18 sets urgent!"

00:01:43.296 --> 00:01:47.868
is just text. A formula can't do
maths with it, and an agent has to

00:01:47.908 --> 00:01:49.121
guess what you meant.

00:01:50.294 --> 00:01:52.219
That brings us to data types.

00:01:52.831 --> 00:01:55.119
Every column holds one type of thing.

00:01:55.731 --> 00:01:57.431
Quantity holds numbers.

00:01:58.044 --> 00:02:02.144
Names hold text. Active
holds true or false.

00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:06.319
And dates hold real dates, not
text that looks like a date.

00:02:07.496 --> 00:02:12.206
I select the date column, then
Format, Number, Custom date and time,

00:02:12.246 --> 00:02:13.709
and pick year-month-day.

00:02:14.321 --> 00:02:20.094
Why? Because "03/04" 04 means
the 3rd of April in Mexico, where

00:02:20.134 --> 00:02:23.471
SafeHands is, and March
the 4th here in the US.

00:02:24.084 --> 00:02:29.856
2026-11-16 can't be misread by
anyone, human or machine, and it sorts

00:02:29.896 --> 00:02:30.734
correctly.

00:02:31.898 --> 00:02:34.236
Now let's make the table do some work.

00:02:34.848 --> 00:02:39.908
In the status column I type: equals
IF, open bracket, D2 less than or

00:02:39.948 --> 00:02:45.196
equal to F2, comma, "REORDER" in
quotes, comma, "OK" in quotes,

00:02:45.236 --> 00:02:49.546
close bracket. In English: if
quantity on hand is at or below the

00:02:49.586 --> 00:02:53.273
reorder point, say
REORDER, otherwise say OK.

00:02:54.452 --> 00:03:00.190
I drag it down. Pallet jack
wheels, 18 against 25: REORDER.

00:03:00.802 --> 00:03:06.865
Corrugated boxes, 1,180
against 1,500: REORDER.

00:03:07.477 --> 00:03:11.552
Hex bolts, 145 against 80: OK.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:16.357
That formula only works because
quantity and reorder point are clean

00:03:16.397 --> 00:03:20.834
numbers. If D2 said "18
sets", this would break.

00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:24.359
Clean data is what makes
the smart stuff possible.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.833
One last bit of magic, for
my fellow Excel lovers.

00:03:29.445 --> 00:03:33.020
The stock log only stores the
supplier ID, not the name.

00:03:33.633 --> 00:03:36.270
But I can look up the name
from the Suppliers table.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:42.205
In K2: equals XLOOKUP, H2, then
the Suppliers ID column, then the

00:03:42.245 --> 00:03:43.795
Suppliers name column.

00:03:44.408 --> 00:03:47.070
Drag down. There they are.

00:03:48.242 --> 00:03:50.642
This is what makes it a real database.

00:03:51.255 --> 00:03:53.267
The name lives in one place only.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.015
If PackRight renames itself tomorrow,
Sarah changes it once, and every

00:03:58.055 --> 00:04:00.117
stock log row updates instantly.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.855
In database language, this ID
connection is called a relationship.

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:06.780
You've just built one.
